    "summary": "Epic Dark Star marathon, scorching Caution > Who Do You Love, tight bandplay",
    "blurb": "The Rotterdam Civic Hall show shines with a near‑45‑minute Dark Star that morphs into a blistering Caution > Who Do You Love sequence. Highlights also include soaring Good Lovin', a powerful Jack Straw and a crisp Sugar Magnolia, all captured on excellent soundboard sources.",
    "review": "May 11, 1972 at Rotterdam Civic Hall captures the Grateful Dead in peak Europe‑72 form. The first set rolls through Pigpen‑fuelled numbers like Black Throated Wind and a rousing Good Lovin', but it’s the second set that cements this night’s legend. A marathon Dark Star – clocking close to 45 minutes – erupts into a seamless drum break, re‑emerges for another stretch of space, then detonates into an explosive Caution (Do Not Stop On Tracks) > Who Do You Love run before cruising home on Sugar Magnolia and Uncle John's Band. Jerry Garcia’s soaring, gain‑heavy wah tones drive the improvisations while Phil Lesh lays down thunderous bass bombs that lock in with Bill Kreutzmann’s propulsive drumming. Keith Godchaux’s organ, initially a touch distant, comes forward to add jazzy texture during the second‑set jams, and Bob Weir provides solid rhythm guitar and vocal punch on Jack Straw. The show is captured by several superb soundboard recordings, with the Ashley/Bertha source delivering crystal‑clear separation that lets every nuance of this high‑energy marathon shine.",
